      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the magnitude of a complex number \\( z = a + bi \\), we use the formula \\( |z| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nGiven the complex number \\( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{8}i \\), we have \\( a = \\frac{1}{2} \\) and \\( b = -\\frac{3}{8} \\).\n\nFirst, we square the real part and the imaginary part:\n\n\\[\na^2 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb^2 = \\left( -\\frac{3}{8} \\right)^2 = \\frac{9}{64}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add these squares together:\n\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{9}{64}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 4 and 64 is 64. So we convert \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) to a fraction with a denominator of 64:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{16}{64}\n\\]\n\nNow we can add the fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{9}{64} = \\frac{16}{64} + \\frac{9}{64} = \\frac{25}{64}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of the sum to find the magnitude:\n\n\\[\n|z| = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{64}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{64}} = \\frac{5}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the magnitude of the complex number \\( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{8}i \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}\n\\]",
